<title>RUSSIA: AGROKHLEB-Russian spring wheat area seen new high.</title>
<headline>AGROKHLEB-Russian spring wheat area seen new high.</headline>
<text>
<p>The following is a direct contribution from SovEcon Ltd, an independent research organisation specialising in agricultural production and trade in the former Soviet Union.</p>
<p>RUSSIAN SPRING WHEAT SOWING ENDS, RECORD LEVEL AREA EXPECTED</p>
<p>MOSCOW, June 17 - The total area sown to spring wheat in Russia is forecast at a new record level of about 16.8 million hectares, compared to 16.4 million hectares last year.</p>
<p>The area increase, combined with good development of winter wheat plantings, is likely to result in a rise in total wheat output in 1997.</p>
<p>SovEcon's forecast for Russia's total wheat output this year remains unchanged in the range of 35-38 million tonnes (1996: 34.9 million tonnes).</p>
<p>By mid-June, Russia had nearly completed her spring wheat sowing campaign.</p>
<p>Most major producing areas are expected to show increased spring wheat areas, particularly those along the Volga River.</p>
<p>In Siberia, wheat plantings are expected to decline against the previous year but to be above average.</p>
<p>In addition, early spring wheat sowing in Siberia may result in higher yields by reducing losses which usually obtain due to adverse weather during the harvest.</p>
